# Focus Desk

Status: In Progress. Review approval pending.

A personal task and focus management app. The project requires at least four independently recorded feature conversations and at least fifty evidence-linked annotations.

## Feature sequence

1. D1: Task workspace — create, edit, complete, delete, search and filter tasks; priority, due date and estimate; local persistence.
2. D2: Focus sessions — task-linked countdown, pause/resume, finish/cancel, reload recovery and retained session history.
3. D3: Daily plan — dated task selection, ordering, daily minute budget, planned workload and completion progress.
4. D4: Insights and export — date-range summaries from retained focus history, task breakdown and CSV export.

Open each feature in a separate original conversation and finish implementation and verification before beginning the next. Keep fixes in the feature's original conversation. Preserve actual message timestamps, IDs, roles and text. Do not synthesize conversation messages or backdate records.

The combined annotation export follows ../conversation-review/recording-workflow.md with a minimum of 50 questions. Reference percentages are planning guidance only; every category and answer needs source support. Preserve source records separately from product files.

Use a local browser app with no account requirement. Keep the implementation within this project directory. Do not publish or alter other projects. The final review note must begin with Ready for Review and link actual validation evidence. Reviewer is not yet designated.
# Conversation and annotation requirements

Status: In Progress. Final review remains pending.

## Feature conversations

The new Mahjong project requires 10 features, each developed in its own original conversation (D1–D10). Features 3, 4, and 5 were implemented and verified sequentially. Features 6–9 have been implemented and verified in D6–D9. Feature 10 has now been implemented and checked in D10. All ten original conversations and 100 questions have been reviewed for format, source grounding and distribution. Keep further Feature 10 fixes in D10. Review approval remains pending.

Open a new development conversation before starting each new feature. Continue that feature's clarification, implementation, testing, and fixes within its conversation. A later feature may depend on earlier work and should state that dependency naturally. An annotation review is not a new feature session.

Preserve each original conversation ID. Assign D1, D2, D3, and subsequent IDs in chronological order. Number messages from 1 within each conversation. Never split a historical conversation by turn, heading, or feature name and present the pieces as separately recorded conversations. Existing conversations that bundled features require fresh, separately recorded development if the study requires one feature per conversation.

## Timing and interaction

Record the actual timestamp of every message, with its original timezone or epoch value. Write the start of each conversation to `session_N_date_time`. Preserve per-message timestamps and original message IDs in a separate source map so the combined file retains the supplied sample's message fields.

Read the previous response, inspect the relevant result, and perform any claimed checks before sending the next request. Leave time for those activities and for composing the message. Long reviews and interaction checks need more time than a short acknowledgment. Do not send a prepared sequence immediately after each response or impose identical pauses on every turn. Waiting by itself does not establish that a check occurred.

Do not alter old timestamps, insert invented delays, or claim to have clicked, read, tested, or reproduced something that did not occur. Keep a timing review that compares each substantive request with the preceding response and the work it describes. Timestamp gaps alone do not establish who performed the interaction or whether sufficient review occurred.

## Combined format

The top level is an array. Each project has exactly `sample_id`, `dataset`, `num_sessions`, `conversation`, and `qa`. Within `conversation`, use paired `session_N_date_time` and `session_N` fields. Each message has `role`, `dia_id`, and `text`.

Each annotation has `question`, `category`, `evidence`, and `answer`. `category` is an array of text labels. Use the sample's spelling: `single-session`, `multi-session`, `singlehop`, `multihop`, `preference`, `temporal`, `knowledge-facts`, and `open-domain`. Evidence refers to the appropriate `Dnumber:message` and may include an exact excerpt. Preserve original message content and speaker roles.

## Question quality and distribution

Use the guide's categories and the supplied combined sample's observed distribution. The PDF does not state numerical category quotas. The 200 sample questions have overlapping labels: 79% single-session, 21% multi-session, 52.5% singlehop, 39% multihop, 17% preference, 14.5% temporal, 25.5% knowledge-facts, and 8% open-domain. These proportions are a planning reference, not exclusive buckets summing to 100%.

For a 100-question set, aim approximately for 21 cross-session questions, 39 multi-hop questions, 15 temporal questions, 17 preference questions, 26 knowledge-fact questions, and 8 open-domain questions, with about 53 single-hop questions. Adjust only for documented source coverage and review; do not fill a quota by adding an unsupported label.

Review the full conversation before writing questions. Cross-session questions must actually require evidence from multiple conversations. Multi-hop questions must combine information to answer; multiple citations alone are insufficient, and multiple facts may occur in one message. Temporal means questions about time: dates, times, durations, elapsed intervals, deadlines, or temporal relationships. Do not label ordinary implementation changes as temporal merely because one version preceded another. Use actual recorded timestamps or explicit time-related content as evidence when applicable. Preference questions should identify a requested choice or constraint. Knowledge-fact and open-domain labels need substantive review against the guide and examples. Keep closed answers brief and open-domain answers to one sentence.

Multi-hop reasoning may also depend on the answers to other questions. For such annotations, preserve the underlying conversation evidence and keep the dependency chain explicit in review notes without adding fields to the sample's combined schema.

The sample has no `adversarial` label. Existing false-premise questions must be reviewed separately; they cannot substitute for missing reference categories. Preserve unresolved labels in a working copy until that review is done.

## Acceptance and review

Validate session boundaries, original IDs and timestamps, citation targets, exact excerpts, category arrays, and the comparison with reference proportions. Distinguish formatting validation from semantic question review. Reaching 100 questions alone is insufficient.

Keep the task In Progress until review approval. A review comment starts with Ready for Review and identifies the precise review scope, revision or local files, validation results, relevant screenshots, and unresolved work. Tag the designated reviewer when known. Do not infer approval from a passing validation command.
